WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.844
Every AI app eventually meets the security review.

2
00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:11.669
And every team answers the same questions: who can call it, where the data is stored, what reaches the model, and who did what.

3
00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:16.615
AI App Server answers them once, in the platform, for every app that runs on it.

4
00:00:17.695 --> 00:00:23.156
We'll walk through five areas: sign-in, encryption, the audit log, guardrails, and secrets.

5
00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:26.459
But first, what happens before you configure anything.

6
00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:28.989
The server starts safe.

7
00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:33.039
It listens only on the local machine until you choose otherwise.

8
00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:38.027
The command line talks to it through a socket in the data folder, not over the network.

9
00:00:38.407 --> 00:00:43.207
A new version of an app must start and pass a health check before it serves any traffic.

10
00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:47.917
And each app runs in its own processes, and can't read another app's data.

11
00:00:48.997 --> 00:00:50.725
Access control comes next.

12
00:00:51.105 --> 00:00:56.972
Create an API key for an app, and from that moment every caller needs a key or a sign-in token.

13
00:00:57.352 --> 00:00:59.528
Without one, the request is refused.

14
00:00:59.908 --> 00:01:04.516
Keys carry roles, and routes can require a role, such as refunds or approver.

15
00:01:04.896 --> 00:01:09.077
Keys are stored hashed: a lost key can't be shown again, only replaced.

16
00:01:10.157 --> 00:01:19.843
For people, apps accept sign-in from Microsoft Entra ID, or any OpenID Connect provider, such as Okta, Auth0, Keycloak or Google.

17
00:01:20.223 --> 00:01:27.924
The server checks each token's signature, issuer, audience and expiry, and fetches and rotates the signing keys itself.

18
00:01:28.304 --> 00:01:32.613
Groups or app roles from your identity provider map onto the app's roles.

19
00:01:32.993 --> 00:01:34.785
And routes require those roles.

20
00:01:35.165 --> 00:01:41.800
Built-in pages can sign people in themselves, with PKCE and sealed cookies, and CSRF protection.

21
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:49.327
One honest note: Entra ID sign-in is tested against a fake of its APIs so far, and live checks are planned.

22
00:01:50.407 --> 00:01:51.708
Second: encryption.

23
00:01:52.088 --> 00:01:59.939
Everything the server stores is encrypted: documents, vectors, memory, jobs, events, secrets, and the audit log itself.

24
00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:06.164
It uses AES-256-GCM, with keys derived from one master key.

25
00:02:06.544 --> 00:02:12.709
Logs and traces never contain prompts or answers, only names, ids, sizes and timings.

26
00:02:13.089 --> 00:02:18.252
Back up the master key separately from the data, and on Kubernetes, keep it in a Secret.

27
00:02:19.332 --> 00:02:20.804
Third: the audit log.

28
00:02:21.184 --> 00:02:26.261
Every model call is recorded with the app, model, tokens, cost and duration.

29
00:02:26.641 --> 00:02:33.788
Guardrail decisions are recorded too, with the reason. The newest record shows a prompt-injection attempt being blocked.

30
00:02:34.168 --> 00:02:39.117
Records can be exported to your SIEM, and the console shows them to auditors and admins.

31
00:02:40.197 --> 00:02:41.563
Fourth: guardrails.

32
00:02:41.943 --> 00:02:50.391
pii-redaction removes emails, phone numbers, card numbers and other personal data before anything is sent to a model provider.

33
00:02:50.771 --> 00:02:58.685
Others redact personal data in answers, block topics, limit input size, or withhold answers that cite no document.

34
00:02:59.065 --> 00:03:04.463
prompt-injection refuses messages that try to override the instructions, like this one.

35
00:03:04.843 --> 00:03:09.963
For more, guardrails can call Microsoft Presidio, or Azure AI Content Safety.

36
00:03:11.043 --> 00:03:12.259
Fifth: secrets.

37
00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:18.719
Provider keys never belong in app files. aiapp secret set stores them encrypted on the server.

38
00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:20.613
Apps refer to them by name.

39
00:03:20.993 --> 00:03:26.924
Or keep them in Azure Key Vault or HashiCorp Vault, where your security team already manages them.

40
00:03:27.304 --> 00:03:34.195
HashiCorp Vault is tested against a real Vault server; Azure Key Vault against a fake of its API, for now.

41
00:03:35.275 --> 00:03:39.051
The docs include a short checklist for opening a server to the network.

42
00:03:39.431 --> 00:03:45.105
Require credentials on every app, put HTTPS in front, and back up the master key.

43
00:03:45.485 --> 00:03:49.688
Then choose guardrails and quotas for each app, and export the audit log.

44
00:03:50.068 --> 00:03:54.484
That's the security model: one review of the platform, instead of one per app.

45
00:03:55.564 --> 00:04:01.025
Next in this series: hardware support, from a laptop CPU to GPU servers.

46
00:04:01.405 --> 00:04:08.360
AI App Server is free to use, and its SDKs, example apps and templates are Apache 2.0.

47
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:15.268
Download it and follow the ten-minute quickstart at github.com/Sandaal/ai-app-server.

